WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.060
- So, as you might've heard,
Florida passed this law

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:04.320
that attempted to prevent
social media platforms

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.920
from deplatforming political candidates

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:07.960
and political speech.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.050
Not surprisingly, industry
groups have already pushed back

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.470
and they're almost certainly going to win,

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:12.690
for at least two reasons.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.680
First of all, Section 230,

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.600
the infamous Communications Decency Act,

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.870
Section 230 explicitly
allows social media platforms

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.150
to moderate their own content.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.740
And, most importantly, that
law preempts state law,

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:26.220
it's a federal law that effectively

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.320
prevents states from
passing laws like this.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.020
And then second of all,
there is the First Amendment.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.210
These are private companies
and they are largely in charge

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:37.400
of what speech they allow or
don't allow on their platforms.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.170
So it's unlikely that this Florida law

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:40.440
is going to last very long

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.390
because we're going to see it in court.

